edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "388c57e6-e07f-5b15-a475-f9e96568702d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259589 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259593 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259593 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259593 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259593 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $2x=\\pi {y}^{3}$ for $y\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259593 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc34530a-2e34-53d7-8fcc-b9ff1814815f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259593 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324496 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\pi }^{5}-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324496 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324496 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "41011d94-bc36-5377-837a-407beee2c7d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324856 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324856 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324856 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69c13dff-cd6b-59c8-8afc-6e8f8eabf8f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325032 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325032 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325032 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7e0e1662-5531-5797-9a2d-180d1aefa39b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325474 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}\\operato